## 2. Trigger.dev (TypeScript)

### Mục tiêu

Trigger.dev là open-source platform cho background jobs và webhook processing với focus trên developer experience và reliability. Trong Cursor Enterprise Framework, Trigger.dev được sử dụng cho serverless background job processing, webhook handling, và scheduled tasks. Mục tiêu của rule này là hướng dẫn developers trong việc implement Trigger.dev workflows một cách hiệu quả và scalable.

Trigger.dev provides a TypeScript-first approach to background jobs với built-in observability và retry mechanisms, làm cho nó ideal cho modern serverless architectures.

### Nguyên tắc

**1. Job Design**

Define clear job responsibilities. Small, focused jobs. Composable tasks. Reusable job templates.

**2. Trigger Selection**

Choose appropriate triggers. Webhooks for events. Schedules for cron. Manual for on-demand.

**3. Payload Handling**

Validate payloads. Transform data. Handle idempotency. Store for debugging.

**4. Error Handling**

Use try-catch blocks. Define retry policies. Implement dead letter handling. Log errors clearly.

**5. Concurrency Control**

Set concurrency limits. Batch processing. Rate limiting. Resource management.

**6. Observability**

Use logging. Track metrics. Monitor success rates. Alert on failures.

**7. Testing**

Test jobs in isolation. Mock external services. Test retry logic. Integration tests.

**8. Security**

Validate webhooks. Use secrets. Rate limiting. Input sanitization.

**9. Monitoring**

Track job execution. Monitor latency. Alert on errors. Dashboard for overview.

**10. Versioning**

Version job definitions. Handle migrations. Maintain backwards compatibility. Document changes.

### Checklist

- [ ] Job requirements defined
- [ ] Trigger types selected
- [ ] Error handling implemented
- [ ] Retry policies configured
- [ ] Testing strategy defined
- [ ] Monitoring set up
- [ ] Documentation created
- [ ] Security reviewed
- [ ] Deployment configured

### Anti Pattern

**1. Large Monolithic Jobs**

Everything in one job. Hard to test. Hard to maintain. Break into smaller jobs.

**2. No Error Handling**

Ignoring failures. Silent errors. Data loss. Proper error handling.

**3. Blocking Operations**

Long-running synchronous operations. Timeout issues. Async when possible.

**4. Hardcoded Secrets**

Secrets in code. Exposed credentials. Use environment variables.

